{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And he dreamed, and behold, a ladder set up on the earth, etc.</b> The Rav haMoreh wrote that the Holy One, blessed be He, explained to Jacob the order of the chain of progression of the levels of three worlds: for in saying 'set up on the earth (אַרְצָה),' this is the nethermost world; 'and its top reaching to heaven,' this is the intermediate world; 'and behold, angels of Elohim ascending and descending on it,' this is the world of angels; 'and behold, YHWH stood above it,' to indicate that HaShem, blessed be He, is exalted above all that is exalted and rules over them all. And Maharai Abravanel raised an objection to his words, for this vision to Jacob is not fitting for the time and not fitting for the place. And I say to vindicate the words of the Moreh that this vision is neither outside its proper time nor outside its proper place, for it is well known what the early authorities wrote, and so you will find in the Akeidah and in Rabbi Bachye, and so Maharai Abravanel himself wrote — that even the sages of the nations agreed upon this — that the three parts of the Mishkan correspond to three worlds, as you will find all explained well in the books of the Geonim that we cited. And according to this design it is certainly in its proper place here, for Jacob was at the site of Mount Moriah, which had been prepared from of old to build the Temple upon it; therefore HaShem showed him in this vision the pattern of three worlds and their design, to hint that in the future the Temple would be built here according to the pattern of the design of these three worlds." "Another matter: therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, showed him the pattern of all the worlds, to indicate that their source is from this place, which is called the Even Shetiyah, for from there the world was founded. Another matter: because from there the efflux goes forth to all the worlds, for the lower beings add power to the strength above, as it were, and therefore the angels of Elohim ascend first, for from there they draw the waters of the efflux from the wellsprings of salvation, and afterward they descend to bestow from above downward. And this secret is alluded to in the words of Rashi on the Song of Songs at the verse 'How beautiful are your steps' (Song of Songs 7:2), that Israel praise the Holy One, blessed be He, from above downward, and the Holy One, blessed be He, counts their praise from below upward, in the manner that He began here with the ladder 'set up on the earth (אַרְצָה)' from below upward — ״וּכְבוֹד אֱלֹהִים הַסְתֵּר דָּבָר״ (Proverbs 25:2). And I wonder at Maharai Abravanel, who wrote that this vision is not in its proper place here — and all the more so according to the words of the Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 68:12), which says: 'and behold, a ladder' — this is the ramp; 'set up on the earth' — this is the altar, etc. You have seen according to all opinions that the matter of the ladder alludes to the Temple, in which Jacob lodged that night." "And further: in the Temple, there sat the thrones of the Sanhedrin, from which Torah would go forth; and the wisdom acquired by a person comes through graded steps, for at first the investigator of intelligibles must recognize the nature of the beings below, and this is 'a ladder set up on the earth' (Genesis 28:12). And from there one ascends to a level greater than this, which is to recognize the nature of the beings in the intermediate world, in the course of the spheres and their motion, and this is what it says: 'and its top reaching to heaven' (Genesis 28:12). And from there one ascends to understand the nature of the separate intellects in the supernal world — and this is what it says: 'and behold, angels of Elohim ascending and descending on it' (Genesis 28:12). And he mentioned 'ascending and descending' because they are four camps of the Shekhinah, as is derived in the Barkaneti on the portion of Balak: Uriel, prince of air; Michael, prince of water; Raphael, prince of dust; Gabriel, prince of fire. And it is known that by nature water and dust descend, and the nature of air and fire is to ascend; therefore he mentioned two ascending and two descending. And from there one ascends to grasp the existence of El, blessed be He — this is what it says: 'and behold, YHWH stood above it' (Genesis 28:13). And according to all these opinions this vision is fitting for the place. However, it is also fitting for the time, for Jacob had fled from Esau, and the fear of him rose upon his head lest he strike him with the sword; therefore they detained him from heaven to pray in the place where his fathers had prayed, for the Temple is called a house of prayer, and a condition had already been stipulated that all the while that Jacob cries out with his voice, the hands of Esau have no dominion over him. And furthermore, the Temple is the opposite of Esau's domain, for Esau's craft is to cut and forge bronze and iron to make from it the sword, and in the Temple it is written (1 Kings 6:7) 'no tool of iron was heard,' and it is written (Deuteronomy 27:5) 'you shall not wield iron upon them,' etc. Therefore they showed Jacob the place of the Temple, to say that through its merit he would be saved from instruments of iron, as He said: 'and behold, I am with you and will keep you wherever you go' (Genesis 28:15)." "And according to the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 68:16), the ladder was set up in Be'er Sheva, and the end of the ladder in Beth El, and the middle of its incline opposite the Temple — this is to indicate that the Temple is the middle point joining the two extremities, the upper ones whose head is in heaven and the lower ones set upon the earth, for there is the dwelling of the Shekhinah with the lower beings, as it is written (Exodus 25:8) 'and I will dwell among them' — that is, within the place that stands in the middle, so as to join the extremities, and they shall be as one on the earth. And some say that the matter of the ladder is to indicate that YHWH stood above it — upon Jacob — and HaShem, blessed be He, made for Himself a dwelling among the lower beings together with all His angels who do His will. Therefore 'ascending' is mentioned first and afterward 'descending,' because their dwelling is below among the lower beings. And regarding the ladder it says 'set earthward (אַרְצָה)' and does not say 'set upon the earth (בָּאָרֶץ),' but rather the feet of the ladder are surely in heaven and it reaches down to the earth; therefore it says אַרְצָה, meaning 'toward the earth (לָאָרֶץ).' And what is written 'and its top (רֹאשׁוֹ) reaching' — every extremity is called רֹאשׁ, and this indicates that through Jacob the world was inverted, and everyone who seeks HaShem will descend below, for there YHWH stands above it."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {3 "The reference 'בָּרְקָנַטִי' appears to designate a specific work or commentator; rendered phonetically as 'the Barkaneti.' The abbreviated 'רז״ל' in comment 3 is resolved as 'our Rabbis of blessed memory,' following standard usage."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:05.000172017Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}